Block end heights in the development log: a testing patch, kept for debugging.

What it does: with DIAG_LOG_LEVEL >= 1 and the "block" feature on, steps.csv gets two more columns:
inner_end_up_mm and outer_end_up_mm, how far each END of the moving block has risen (world z) since the step began;
inner = the end nearer the tower centre.  Plus a unit test (jenga/diaglog.py, tests/test_diaglog.py).

Why: block_tilt_deg says a block tipped, not which way.  The end heights tell whether the block's inner end is
riding up on the layer below or its outer end is sagging out of the hand: that is how the push method's pull
problems (dragging the layer above, tilt on the pedestal) were told apart.  Useful for any pull or place problem.

To use it:   git apply workfiles/end_heights_testing.patch    (independent of the other patches; both can be on)
             then run with DIAG_LOG_LEVEL=1 (cluster scripts: --set DIAG_LOG_LEVEL=1)
Take it out again before committing (git apply -R workfiles/end_heights_testing.patch).
Made against the commit that added this file; check with `git apply --check` first.

